# LF everywhere, on every platform. A contributor whose git has core.autocrlf=true will
# otherwise commit CRLF blobs (chain.mjs, stub.mjs and .env.example each landed
# that way once), which makes every line of those files conflict on the next merge. Scripts
# under backend/scripts/ additionally fail to run at all: bash reads the \r as part of the
# last token on the line, so `set -euo pipefail` becomes an unknown option.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zip binary
*.so binary
